is where a single whole-length search times out): DO NOT hammer parallel_search on the whole target. Instead DECOMPOSE -> DESIGN -> STITCH -> REPAIR: call analyze_target, then for EACH unit in `decomposition.units` run parallel_search on that unit's short `sub_target` INDEPENDENTLY (short => it converges fast); place each solved unit's bases at its `positions`, fill the seam (linkers A-rich, multiloop closing pairs G-C); then RnaVerify the FULL assembly and run the diagnosis-repair loop editing ONLY the diagnosed seam positions -- the units are already correct. This turns one exponential search into many small ones. Always confirm the final sequence with RnaVerify. STRONG SOLVER PRIMITIVES (prefer these over hand-writing search code): mcp__rna_motifs__inverse_fold_search runs MANY parallel ViennaRNA inverse_fold restarts on a target or a unit's sub_target (much stronger at closing pairs than parallel_search; can warm-start from an assembled candidate); mcp__rna_motifs__finish takes a NEAR-MISS (bp_distance 1-3) and hill-climbs the diagnosed mismatched positions to an exact solve. Typical hard-target recipe: analyze_target -> inverse_fold_search each unit's sub_target -> assemble (A-rich linkers, G-C seams) -> RnaVerify -> finish the near-miss. RED LINE (mandatory): you must DESIGN the sequence with these tools. You must NOT web-search for, look up, or copy any published/known Eterna100 answer sequence for this target -- that is forbidden and invalidates the result. Apply general STRATEGY; never retrieve the answer.  SOLVER LADDER — follow IN ORDER; every search must be SYNCHRONOUS (NEVER a background job, NEVER poll a logfile in a loop — that burns the whole time budget):   (a) If length <= ~120 nt: FIRST call mcp__rna_motifs__inverse_fold_search on the WHOLE target — it often solves outright and returns an EXACT-length sequence; submit it VERBATIM.   (b) If long/multiloop: call analyze_target, then inverse_fold_search on EACH unit's sub_target, place each result at